{
  "instrument": "Fluke multimeter (8845A / 8846A)",
  "source": "Transcribed by hand from the Fluke 8845A/8846A Digital Multimeter Programmers Manual (September 2006). The guide's command reference is a set of indented tree tables whose description column pdftotext shifts by a row in places — Table 12 pairs ':DIODe?' with 'Make a frequency measurement' — so these entries were read off and paired by eye rather than extracted, and the ':CURRent:VOLTage' style nonsense an automatic pass produced was discarded. Cross-checked against pymeasure fluke and the Keysight 34401A set, which this meter follows closely. A guide sweep reports 46 entries as absent; all 46 are placeholders this catalog writes out. The guide prints MEASure[:SCALar][:<function>]? and CONFigure[:SCALar][:<function>] once each, and [SENSe:]<function>:RANGe(?), :RANGe:AUTO(?), :RESolution(?) and :NPLCycles(?) once each, with <function> filled from the list under [SENSe]:FUNCtion — VOLTage:AC, CURRent:DC, RESistance, FREQuency and the rest. Expanding those gives 24 and 20 entries; the optional [:SCALar] node is dropped and NPLCycles is written short as NPLC. The last two are [SENSe:]FUNCtion1 and its query, which the guide prints as [SENSe]:FUNCtion[1/2] — the colon outside the bracket, and the display selector as a choice rather than the digit. None of that is a command missing from the guide, and the 2006 text layer is not the reason: the reference chapter extracts cleanly, whatever it does to the description columns.",
  "manufacturer": "Fluke",
  "guide": { "title": "8845A/8846A Digital Multimeter Programmers Manual", "edition": "September 2006", "vendor": "Fluke", "url": "https://www.fluke.com/en-us/support/manuals", "fileName": "Fluke_8845A_8846A_Multimeter_ProgrammersManual.pdf" },
